hell commands use `pwsh.exe`. Plugin-owned hooks may use the runner declared by that plugin contract. - Codex plugin tools/hooks must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Codex`; Claude plugin tools/hooks must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Claude`; Copilot plugin tools/hooks must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Copilot`; Cline MCP tools must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Cline`; Grok plugin skills/hooks/MCP server declarations must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Grok`. - For Grok, the Streamable HTTP MCP server exposes tool names such as `sessionlog_*`, `todo_*`, `requirements_*`, and memory tools when available; hosted-agent `mcp_*` names are not expected from that transport. `workflow.sessionlog.*`, `workflow.todo.*`, `workflow.memory.*`, and `workflow.requirements.*` are plugin shim/REPL method names used by the Grok skills and `repl-invoke` helpers; they are not expected to appear as literal `search_tool` results. - Local plugin root hints in `agent_plugins.agents.<Agent>.root_hints` are fallback verification hints after tool-registry acquisition, not the primary install source. - Claude Code agents must trigger the `claude-hook-validation` skill during marker/plugin bootstrap. That skill validates active Claude settings for MCP `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop`, and `PostToolUse` hook commands from `hooks/hooks.json`; if any required hook is missing, it uses `install-claude-mcp-hooks.ps1` from the `claude-hook-wiring` skill to merge MCP hooks into active Claude settings without replacing existing hooks. Restart Claude Code after the skill reports installed hooks.\r\n205: \r\n206:   ## Model-Authored Session Logging\r\n207:   - Models must write MCP Session Log turns, dialog, actions, decisions, and completions themselves through the active agent's session-log surface (`workflow.sessionlog.*`, `sessionlog_*`, `mcp_session_*`, or equivalent plugin tool).\r\n208:   - Plugins must not ingest local chat transcripts to populate normal session logs and must not expose transcript-ingestion helpers, skills, endpoint shortcuts, or parser forks.\r\n209:   - Transcript ingestion and normalization are explicit non-plugin server/client/REPL/MCP operations. Use them only when the user asks to import or normalize transcript files, not as a substitute for live model-authored logging.\r\n210:   ## MCP Requirements Visibility\r\n211:   - When implementing or enforcing requirements, query current effective requirements for the active workspace layer through the active agent's requirements surface (`workflow.requirements.effective`, `client.Requirements.GetEffectiveRequirementsAsync`, `req_effective`, or equivalent plugin tool). Use the layer catalog surface (`workflow.requirements.listLayers/createLayer/updateLayer`, `client.Requirements.ListRequirementLayersAsync/CreateRequirementLayerAsync/UpdateRequirementLayerAsync`, `req_list_layers/req_create_layer/req_update_layer`, or equivalent plugin tools) when the user asks to inspect or manage requirement layers. Do not treat raw all-requirements lists, exported markdown, or future-layer requirements as currently enforceable unless the user explicitly asks to inspect or plan those layers.\r\n212:   ## YAML Mutation Rule\r\n213: \r\n214:   - Never edit YAML files by appending, replacing, or removing text lines. This includes one-key configuration changes.\r\n215: \r\n216:   - Always deserialize the complete document into an object, mutate the object, serialize it, and save the serialized result.\r\n217: \r\n218:   - For PowerShell work, use `plugins/core/lib-ps/yaml-object-mutation.ps1` and call `Set-McpYamlObjectValue` or `Update-McpYamlObject`. Do not build YAML snippets as strings or use line-oriented edit helpers for YAML.\r\n219: \r\n220:   ## MCP and Plugin Failure Reporting\r\n221:   - MCP Server failures and required-plugin failures discovered while working must always be written as a normal failsafe YAML report. Use the plugin/REPL failsafe or pending YAML queue and preserve the triage method (`workflow.triage.report`, `triage_report`, or equivalent plugin triage tool) plus title, summary, observed behavior, expected behavior, severity, component, workspace path, dedupe key or error signature, affected paths, evidence, reproduction hints, tags, reporter agent, session id, turn id, and current TODO id when known.\r\n222:   - After the failsafe YAML is written and triage submission succeeds, continue the user's active task. Do not wait for triage research, TODO creation, or resolution.\r\n223:   - Do not create TODOs, requirements, GitHub issues, manual repair plans, or alternate reports for MCP Server/plugin failures unless the user's active request is to fix that failure.\r\n224:   - If the MCP Server or required-plugin failure reveals that code, docs, requirements, plugin skills, package sync, deployment, configuration, or other artifacts need to be updated as a separate repair workflow, write a detailed triage report that includes the needed changes, acceptance criteria, and validation evidence, then continue the active task after the failsafe YAML is written and triage submission succeeds.\r\n225:   - If triage submission fails, stop work and notify the user. Keep the failsafe YAML report for later replay; do not use raw REST or another reporting channel as a substitute.\r\n226: \r\n227:   ## Agent Help (MCP Server issues)\r\n228:   When you are stuck on any MCP Server surface (marker trust, plugin bootstrap, session log, TODO, requirements, triage, memory, context search, federation, hooks, or API behavior), request expert help instead of improvising raw REST bypasses.\r\n229:   - **When to invoke:** signature or health nonce failures you cannot resolve; unexpected tool or plugin behavior; `MCP_PLUGIN_UNAVAILABLE`; repeated 401/503 after re-reading this marker; documentation contradictions.\r\n230:   - **How to invoke:** preferred `workflow.agenthelp.createSession` or MCP tool `agent_help_create_session` with caller linkage (`callerAgent`, `callerSessionId`, `callerRequestId`) and a factual issue summary (observation vs inference separated). REST: `POST /mcpserver/agent-help/session` then WebSocket `/mcpserver/agent-help/session/{id}/stream` (SSE fallback: `POST .../turn/stream`).\r\n231:   - **Caller obligations:** include reproduction steps, exact error text, and files already consulted. Do not instruct the helper to ignore guardrails, skip triage, or mutate TODO/session-log/requirements storage directly.\r\n232:   - Agent help is for diagnosis and coordination, not a substitute for the required plugin route for normal MCP work.\r\n233: \r\n234:   ## MCP Memories\r\n235:   MCP memories are durable operator guidance managed by the MCP Server. Global memories apply to every workspace; Workspace memories apply only to the active workspace. Memory IDs use `MEMORY-{CATEGORY}-{NNN}`.\r\n236:   Agents that support request-boundary injection must render required memories exactly as:\r\n237:   REQUIRED MEMORIES - MEMORY-REQ-001: Raw memory text.\r\n238:   If no required memories are visible, render:\r\n239:   REQUIRED MEMORIES - None.\r\n240:   Preserve raw memory text exactly; do not summarize, paraphrase, reorder within a scope, silently generate new memories, or add secrets. MCP memories are not a secret store. Effective memory order is Global memories first sorted by ID, then current Workspace memories sorted by ID.\r\n241:   Existing agent-local memory stores are caches or migration sources only; the MCP memory store is the authoritative shared record. Import agent-local memories only when the operator approves the scope, the content belongs to this workspace or is intentionally Global, and the import does not leak secrets, private notes, or unrelated workspace guidance.\r\n242:   Attribute memory mutations with the real agent or user identity via `updatedBy` when supported, and record successful memory add/update/remove operations in the active session log through `workflow.sessionlog.appendActions`. Keep import provenance in the session log or dialog instead of adding private source paths to memory text.\r\n243:   Manage memories through the required plugin or MCP tool surface: `memory_add`, `memory_list`, `memory_update`, `memory_remove`; REPL `workflow.memory.add`, `workflow.memory.list`, `workflow.memory.update`, `workflow.memory.remove`; or REST `/mcpserver/memory` when explicitly allowed for non-plugin diagnostics.\r\n244: \r\n245:   ## Codex MCP Quickstart\r\n\u001b[1m==> C:\\Users\\kingd\\.codex\\plugins\\cache\\mcpserver-codex-plugin\\mcpserver\\1.106.0\\skills\\workflow\\SKILL.md <==\u001b[0m\r\n  1: ---\r\n  2: name: MCP-First Workflow\r\n  3: description: This skill should be used when working in a repository that uses McpServer for session continuity, TODOs, requirements, and device validation.\r\n  4: ---\r\n  5: ## Goal\r\n  6: \r\n  7: Minimize repeated context reconstruction by using McpServer as the primary continuity source.\r\n  8: \r\n  9: ## Preferred Workflow\r\n 10: \r\n 11: 1. Read current task/session state first.\r\n 12: 2. Read recent checkpoints or deltas only if needed.\r\n 13: 3. Read TODOs or requirements only when they materially affect the current task.\r\n 14: 4. Inspect local code only when implementation work is required.\r\n 15: 5. For attached Android validation, use `adb_step` for screenshot -> inspect -> act -> screenshot loops.\r\n 16: 6. Record a checkpoint after meaningful progress, failure, or a change in plan.\r\n 17: \r\n 18: ## Rules\r\n 19: \r\n 20: - Prefer the smallest MCP query that can answer the question.\r\n 21: - Prefer deltas/checkpoints over full history.\r\n 22: - Do not ask the user to restate recent work if session logs should already contain it.\r\n 23: - Use MCP for continuity and mechanics.\r\n 24: - Keep reasoning, planning, and coding decisions in Codex.\r\n 25: \r\n 26: ## Anti-Patterns\r\n 27: \r\n 28: Avoid:\r\n 29: - broad context reads before checking session state\r\n 30: - asking the user to manually test when `adb_step` can perform the validation\r\n 31: - reading large log histories when a recent checkpoint or delta would suffice"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
